<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Network. Previously undeclared: the app only reached the network because
      ktor/okhttp merge INTERNET in from their library manifests. Declared here so
      the dependency is explicit rather than inherited.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Live-agent VoIP audio (Voximplant human-agent leg — monitor / takeover).
      RECORD_AUDIO is runtime-sensitive and requested at the live-supervision surface
      (telephony/CallAudioPermissions); the console genuinely carries two-way voice on
      takeover, not for convenience.

      MANAGE_OWN_CALLS is no longer absent, and is no longer declared by hand: the
      androidx.core:core-telecom AAR declares it in its own manifest and merges it in
      (verified by unpacking core-telecom-1.0.1.aar, not read from docs), together with
      androidx.core.telecom.internal.JetpackConnectionService. AOSP gives
      MANAGE_OWN_CALLS protectionLevel="normal", so it needs no runtime request.

      A CORRECTION THIS BLOCK USED TO GET WRONG, because the wrong version is load-bearing
      for the FGS types below: it said `phoneCall` "additionally requires self-managed
      Telecom (MANAGE_OWN_CALLS + a ConnectionService) OR the default-dialer role". It
      does not. AOSP's own policy table, android.app.ForegroundServiceTypePolicy,
      FGS_TYPE_POLICY_PHONE_CALL, requires FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL plus ANY ONE of
      { RegularPermission(MANAGE_OWN_CALLS), RolePermission(ROLE_DIALER) }. MANAGE_OWN_CALLS
      is a plain permission check, satisfied by the manifest declaration alone at
      protectionLevel="normal" — no registered PhoneAccount, no ConnectionService, no
      CallsManager.addCall. So the core-telecom dependency, not TelecomRegistration's
      runtime call, is what unblocks `phoneCall` here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <!--
      BOTH call FGS types are declared, and the pair is deliberate. Read this before
      dropping either one — the two types are not alternatives, they cover different
      phases of one call, and each alone produces a different failure.

      * `microphone` alone (what shipped before this) CRASHES the push-woken ring.
        FGS_TYPE_POLICY_MICROPHONE carries foregroundOnlyPermission=true, i.e. its
        RECORD_AUDIO prerequisite is while-in-use, so a background start throws
        SecurityException. VoxIncomingCallCoordinator starts the FGS during ringing,
        BEFORE it posts the notification, from a process FCM has just woken — no
        while-in-use exemption applies (high-priority FCM exempts the background START,
        which is a different list from the while-in-use one). That is precisely the
        pocketed-and-locked case this app exists for.
      * `phoneCall` alone fixes that crash and SILENTLY KILLS CALL AUDIO. OomAdjuster
        grants PROCESS_CAPABILITY_FOREGROUND_MICROPHONE only when the microphone bit is
        set in the RUNNING service type; the framework's own comment on
        CAMERA_MICROPHONE_CAPABILITY_CHANGE_ID (@EnabledAfter targetSdk Q, and this app
        targets 36) says a FGS has microphone while-in-use capability "only when the
        foregroundServiceType is configured as ... FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_MICROPHONE".
        An answered call would run with no microphone whenever the app is not visible.

      Declaring both is safe because the manifest attribute is only a CEILING: ActiveServices
      checks that the value passed to startForeground() is a subset of it, and runs each
      type's prerequisite policy over the bits actually PASSED, not over everything declared.
      So the service picks per phase — `phoneCall` while ringing (legal from the background,
      and no microphone is needed yet), then `phoneCall|microphone` on answer, which is
      reached either by a notification action or by the full-screen-intent activity being
      visible, both of which satisfy while-in-use.

      NEVER pass the pair during the ring phase: that re-validates microphone and brings the
      SecurityException straight back. The manifest may hold both; the ring-phase
      startForeground() call may not.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL" />
    <!--
      DO NOT "clean up" the next line. Beyond the locked-screen ring it is named for, it
      is what stops the incoming-call CallStyle notification from THROWING on Android 14+
      devices where the full-screen-intent app-op is off. NotificationManagerService's
      checkDisqualifyingFeatures rejects a CallStyle notification that is neither an
      FGS/UIJ notification nor carrying a full-screen intent — and this app's is neither
      (VoxIncomingCallCoordinator posts it with NotificationManagerCompat.notify, not
      startForeground). When the app-op is off the service strips the fullScreenIntent
      and substitutes FLAG_FSI_REQUESTED_BUT_DENIED, but only for a package that DECLARED
      the permission. Declared: FSI-denied degrades to a heads-up notification. Undeclared:
      IllegalArgumentException and no incoming-call notification at all. (Traced through
      AOSP NotificationManagerService by notification-owner, 2026-08-14.) Removing it
      means moving that notification under startForeground in the same change.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- Android 12+ (API 31) Bluetooth headset routing. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT" />

    <!--
      Presence foreground service (docs/android-presence-and-call-ux.md §1): required
      alongside the specialUse foreground-service type declared below. No closer-fitting
      type exists — the heartbeat has no audio, no location and no bulk data transfer,
      and it is not a call: it runs while an agent is on shift with no leg in progress.

      This note used to end "and `phoneCall` requires the self-managed Telecom this app
      deliberately does not adopt yet". That clause is FALSE twice over now, so it is
      gone rather than left to be repeated: a self-managed PhoneAccount is registered
      (telephony/TelecomRegistration.kt), and `phoneCall` IS now declared and used — by
      CallForegroundService, for actual calls. The presence heartbeat is a different
      service with a different job, and the only argument that ever mattered for it is
      the one above: it is not a call. It runs while an agent is on shift with no leg in
      progress, so `phoneCall` would be a false description of it regardless of what is
      declared. specialUse stands on that alone.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

    <!--
      Permissions the Voximplant SDK merges in that this app must NOT ship.
      Traced to their source in the release manifest-merger report rather than
      guessed, after `gplay preflight` flagged them on the built AAB (2026-08-14).

      CAMERA comes from com.voximplant:android-sdk-calls, which supports video
      calling. This console is voice-only — every callUser in the scenarios passes
      video:false, and no screen offers video — so shipping CAMERA would ask users
      for a capability the app never exercises AND force a photo/video disclosure
      in the Play Data safety form for data that is never collected. Removed here
      rather than left to be explained away.
      IF VIDEO IS EVER ADDED, this removal must go first, or the camera will fail
      to open with a permission error that points nowhere near this file.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE also comes from android-sdk-calls, and
      READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is IMPLIED from it by the merger, not requested by
      anyone. Both are dead weight on a modern target: WRITE has done nothing
      since Android 11, and READ was superseded by READ_MEDIA_* in Android 13.
      Capped rather than removed, because minSdk here is 24 and the SDK may
      genuinely use them on the old API levels where they still mean something —
      the caps are exactly the versions at which the platform stops honouring
      each one, so nothing that works today stops working.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32"
        tools:replace="android:maxSdkVersion" />

    <!--
      Advertising ID: explicitly NOT used. `gplay preflight` warns that an
      ads/attribution SDK is present without AD_ID declared, and the honest
      response is the opposite of what the hint suggests — declaring AD_ID would
      add a sensitive permission and a Data safety disclosure for an identifier
      this app never reads.

      What the scanner detects is `com.google.firebase.analytics.connector.
      AnalyticsConnector`, an interface stub that ships inside firebase-messaging.
      Verified in the built bundle's dex, not inferred: AnalyticsConnector and its
      R class are the ONLY com/google/firebase/analytics/* entries, the real
      entry point `FirebaseAnalytics` appears zero times, and both
      com/google/android/gms/measurement and com/google/android/gms/ads are
      absent. The dependency tree agrees — no firebase-analytics,
      play-services-measurement or ads-identifier artifact anywhere.

      Declared as a removal rather than simply omitted so the position is
      enforced: if a future dependency merges AD_ID in, the build strips it here
      instead of silently shipping it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID" tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- Microphone is required for the console to function; not an optional feature. -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.microphone"
        android:required="true" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.MyApplication">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyApplication">
            <!--
              singleTask (docs/android-presence-and-call-ux.md §3): a locked-device
              incoming-call full-screen intent re-targets this SAME activity via
              onNewIntent instead of stacking a second instance. Single-activity app —
              no back-stack semantics change from this.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Ongoing-call foreground service. Keeps a ringing or answered agent leg alive
          with an ongoing notification. Not exported; started only in-process.

          foregroundServiceType is the CEILING, not the choice — see the long note on the
          two FOREGROUND_SERVICE_* permissions at the top of this file for why both types
          are listed and which phase may pass which. Short version: `phoneCall` while
          ringing (a background start of a `microphone` FGS throws SecurityException on
          Android 14+), then `phoneCall|microphone` on answer (a `phoneCall`-only FGS is
          granted no microphone capability at all).

          Widening this attribute is safe on its own and in any merge order: ActiveServices
          only requires that the value passed to startForeground() be a SUBSET of what is
          declared here, so a service still passing FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_MICROPHONE keeps
          working against this exactly as before. Narrowing it is what breaks — dropping a
          type the service still passes throws IllegalArgumentException on every call.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".telephony.CallForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="phoneCall|microphone" />

        <!--
          Push wake-up (AGENTS.md "Push wake-up"): Voximplant sends a Firebase
          data-message push when callUser is invoked for an agent whose device is
          backgrounded or killed. Not exported — only the FCM system component
          delivers to it, gated by the standard MESSAGING_EVENT action.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".telephony.vox.VoxFirebaseMessagingService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          Presence foreground service (docs/android-presence-and-call-ux.md §1) — the
          fix for AgentPresence.setStatus writing once and never again. specialUse: no
          audio, no location, no bulk data transfer, and — the only ground that still
          matters — not a call. This heartbeat runs while an agent is on shift with NO
          leg in progress, so `phoneCall` would misdescribe it even though that type is
          now declared and used by CallForegroundService next door. The older reason
          given here, "phoneCall needs the self-managed Telecom this app doesn't adopt
          yet", stopped being true when TelecomRegistration landed and is removed rather
          than reworded. Requires a Play Console justification before any Play
          distribution (owner action, not blocking today — no upload keystore yet).
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".telephony.presence.PresenceForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Keeps a call-center agent's on-shift presence heartbeat alive so the server can route inbound and AI-escalated calls to this device while the app is backgrounded" />
        </service>

        <!--
          Notification action receivers (docs §1 and §3). Not exported — only this
          app's own PendingIntents target them.
        -->
        <receiver android:name=".telephony.presence.PresenceActionReceiver" android:exported="false" />
        <receiver android:name=".telephony.vox.IncomingCallActionReceiver" android:exported="false" />
        <receiver android:name=".telephony.CallHangupActionReceiver" android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          No ConnectionService is declared here on purpose, and the hand-written
          ConsoleConnectionService stub that used to sit commented out in this spot has
          been deleted rather than left as a TODO.

          androidx.core:core-telecom supplies androidx.core.telecom.internal.JetpackConnectionService
          — same BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE guard, same android.telecom.ConnectionService
          intent filter — and the manifest merger pulls it in. The stub named a class
          (.telephony.ConsoleConnectionService) that was never written, so nothing is
          lost by deleting it, and AGENTS.md's own recommendation was to prefer the
          Jetpack library over hand-writing one. Registration now happens in
          telephony/TelecomRegistration.kt via CallsManager. Whether a second,
          hand-written ConnectionService could coexist with the Jetpack one is untested
          and deliberately not relied on here.

          Still open, and separate: routing calls through CallsManager.addCall, which
          hands Telecom the call's audio focus and lifecycle and carries a 5s
          CallControlScope deadline that needs a physical device to verify. Note that
          Google's AEP Telecom guideline treats addCall as mandatory for a VoIP app, so
          "registered but never adds calls" is a knowingly non-compliant resting state,
          not a finished one — see telephony/TelecomRegistration.kt's kdoc.

          ── What core-telecom actually merges into the shipped manifest ──
          Read out of the unpacked AAR's own AndroidManifest.xml, so the merged result is
          not a surprise at review time. Nothing below is declared by hand here, and
          nothing below should be tools:node="remove"d without reading why first:

          * uses-permission MANAGE_OWN_CALLS — AOSP protectionLevel="normal" (verified in
            frameworks/base/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml), granted at install, no dialog.
            This is the permission AGENTS.md's "Known state" bullet says not to add for
            the full-screen-intent reason; it arrives via this dependency and the
            contradiction is unresolved — flagged for the owner, see TelecomRegistration.
          * uses-permission BLUETOOTH_CONNECT and MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS — both already
            declared above by this app, so the merge is a no-op.
          * service androidx.core.telecom.internal.JetpackConnectionService,
            exported="true", guarded by BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE (a system
            permission), intent-filter android.telecom.ConnectionService. Exported is
            required: Telecom binds it cross-process. It is never bound today — the
            platform binds a ConnectionService only to create a connection, which is
            addCall's job.
          * receiver androidx.core.telecom.internal.MuteStateReceiver, exported="true",
            intent-filter android.media.action.MICROPHONE_MUTE_CHANGED. Exported without
            a permission guard looks wrong and is not: that action is listed as a
            <protected-broadcast> in AOSP frameworks/base/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml,
            so only the system can send it. Left in place — it backs the library's
            LocalCallSilence extension, removing it buys nothing measurable, and the
            consequence of removing it cannot be verified without a device.
        -->
    </application>

</manifest>
